Turkey and Russia have launched join  ground patrols in northeastern Syria the  operation is part of a deal struck by  the countries to end turkeys offensive  against Kurdish militia and the border  region and removed the Kurdish fighters  from the area the patrols will begin or  within a 10-kilometer zone along the  border the steps immense the power shift  in Syria a month  after President Donald Trump withdrew US  forces from there for more on I joined  by our correspondent Yulia Hahn in  Istanbul you live what are these patrols  all about well the deal struck there  between Ankara and Moscow last week  provided for the withdrawal of Syrian  Kurdish fighters some 30 kilometers from  the Turkish border Ankara considers  these fighters terrorists with the links  to the local PKK insurgency  so the withdrawal was the Turkish  government's condition for pausing it's  a deadly offensive there in northern  Syria now Russia said some 34,000  fighters have indeed left the area but  Turkey wants to use these joint patrols  to confirm this in president Aaron has  repeatedly threatened in the past days  to restart this military operation if  deemed necessary and Iulia Syrian  President Bashar al-assad has been  talking about Turkish Russian  cooperation let's first take a listen to  what he said on a syrian state TV this  agreement is a positive step  it does not achieve everything in the  sense that it will not pressure the  Turks to leave immediately however it  limits the damage and paves the way for  the liberation of this region in the  future or the immediate future as we  hope so yulia assad is sounding very  upbeat there what do you make of his  comments well yes it appears Assad feels  like he's the big winner after  everything we saw happening there in  northern Syria since early October since  US President Trump and now  the withdrawal of American troops from  the area as we know after turkey started  its military operation the Kurds have  turned to the Syrian regime and its most  powerful ally Russia for help against  the Turkish attacks and this in turn  allowed Bashar lasat to swiftly deploy  forces and reclaim territory there in  northern Syria where he didn't have a  foothold in many years now the Kurds in  the area are still somehow hoping for a  political solution for some kind of  self-rule deal with the Syrian regime  but yesterday in his speech Bashar  al-assad said that he aims to restore  state authority over the Kurdish control  areas so for him overall this is a huge  step towards winning the Syrian war  there and that's why expectations are  very low that the Syrian regime will  make any concession concessions for  example in Geneva where currently we  have talks going on about a new Syrian  Constitution brokered by the United  Nations not the UN secretary-general  Guterres mehta turkish President Recep  tibe at dawn today what did they talk  about well nothing has emerged yet  publicly from this meeting but we know  that touch president add one wanted to  talk with Qataris about his plans for  establishing what he calls as a safe  zone there in northern Syria this is one  of the main objectives of turkeys  military operation Aaron wants to  resettle many of the 4.6 million Syrian  refugees currently living here in Turkey  to this area he wants to build houses  schools police stations hospitals so  whole cities basically and this is of  course a very costly project state media  here in Turkey have reported an  estimated 26 billion u.s. dollars at  least this would would cost so Aaron was  going to ask a terrace for setting up an  international donors conference because  he can't pay for it alone the question  is would the UN really get on board  because these so-called safe zone plans  have risk  a lot of criticism internationally  outside of Turkey some say this is  Turkey trying to occupy parts of  northern Syria of Syria or trying to  change the demographics of this area and  then of course the big question is what  do Syrian refugees themselves say what  they voluntary voluntarily return to  this area Yulia Hahn in the Turkish city  of Istanbul thank you very much for that
 

